.TH "GLESHA" "1" "" "glesha" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
glesha \- encrypted archives and indexed cloud backups
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glesha [GLOBAL OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGUMENTS] [OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create standalone encrypted archives or manage named backup sets on AWS S3 and Backblaze B2.
Tar, xz, gzip, bzip2, OpenPGP and SQLite run internally; the installed executable needs no external tools.
Full archives can also be recovered independently with GPG and tar.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B create
Register a backup set and its source paths locally. This creates no archive and makes no cloud requests. See glesha-create(1).
.TP
.B configure
Change a set's saved settings or map imported roots. Previously bound source paths cannot be replaced. See glesha-configure(1).
.TP
.B run
Create and upload a full backup, or an explicit incremental. Repeating a pending run resumes recorded work. See glesha-run(1).
.TP
.B retry
Resume a pending backup, move, catalog publication or storage\-class change. Completed uploads are reused. See glesha-retry(1).
.TP
.B list
Show known sets. Remote discovery registers published set descriptions without downloading backup payloads. See glesha-list(1).
.TP
.B status
Show local backup state. With \-\-refresh, inspect remote classes and retrieval readiness without requesting retrieval. See glesha-status(1).
.TP
.B history
Show completed snapshots, newest first, including transfer and storage status. See glesha-history(1).
.TP
.B browse
Browse a snapshot's file metadata in the local catalog. This makes no cloud requests and downloads no file content. See glesha-browse(1).
.TP
.B restore
Reconstruct a full snapshot in a new destination. Cold retrieval requires separate, explicit consent. See glesha-restore(1).
.TP
.B import
Catalog an existing full archive without changing its encrypted bytes or uploading a replacement payload. See glesha-import(1).
.TP
.B move
Copy and verify the complete set history before switching providers. Existing source copies are retained. See glesha-move(1).
.TP
.B archive
Create a standalone encrypted tar archive. No set name or cloud provider is needed. See glesha-archive(1).
.TP
.B decrypt
Validate an encrypted archive and write its compressed tar to a new output file. See glesha-decrypt(1).
.TP
.B extract
Validate and extract an encrypted or plaintext compressed tar into a new directory. See glesha-extract(1).
.TP
.B upload
Upload an existing file. Replacing an existing object key requires \-\-force. See glesha-upload(1).
.TP
.B download
Download encrypted bytes from explicitly selected providers, trying them in the supplied order. See glesha-download(1).
.TP
.B catalog
Synchronize metadata explicitly. Pull preserves the old local catalog; reconcile preserves conflicting histories; abandon preserves pending data. See glesha-catalog(1).
.TP
.B storage-class
Change an AWS object's storage class with server\-side copy. Archive identity and older versions are retained. See glesha-storage-class(1).
.TP
.B check
Check selected providers using metadata and an owned temporary write/read/delete operation. See glesha-check(1).
.TP
.B help
Show complete command options and examples.
.TP
.B version
Show the executable version and build revision without loading configuration or credentials.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-L STRING
debug, info, warn, error or silent (default "info")
.TP
.B \-\-color STRING
yes, no or auto; always/never aliases accepted (default "auto")
.TP
.B \-\-config STRING
TOML configuration file
.TP
.B \-\-env STRING
explicit credential environment file
.TP
.B \-\-help
show help
.TP
.B \-\-log\-level STRING
debug, info, warn, error or silent (default "info")
.TP
.B \-\-version
show version; \-version is also accepted.
.TP
.B \-v
Alias for \-\-version.
.SH DEFAULTS
Backups are full unless --incremental is explicitly supplied. Archive compression
is xz at level 6; gzip and bzip2 are selectable. Configuration uses CLI values before TOML
before built-in defaults. There is no upload or standalone download provider default.
Supply --to at create time or run time. Comma-separated and repeated selections
are accepted; --from keeps their order for fallback. An incremental set keeps
its entire history on one authoritative provider; move copies history before switching.
XZ levels 1..9 select dictionaries from 64 KiB to 16 MiB, doubling per level;
level 6 uses 2 MiB. These differ from external xz presets. Higher levels may
require a larger --memory-max. Existing sets retain their saved compression;
use configure SET --compression xz to change future runs. Metadata stays gzip.
XZ imports support LZMA2 without additional filters and reject dictionaries
that exceed the decoder memory budget.
.SH SAFETY
Bound source paths cannot be replaced under the same set name. Restore and extract
require a new output directory; archive and download require a new output file.
Incremental removed paths and type replacements require a default-no [y/N] prompt;
--assume-yes permits automation. Raw object replacement requires --force.
Historical snapshots and moved source copies are retained. Cold retrieval is never
implicit: use the retrieval confirmation or --request-retrieval. Bulk is the default;
standard and expedited are explicit opt-ins. Deep Archive does not support expedited.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The default config.toml is under the platform user-config directory in glesha.
Credentials come from process variables or an explicit --env file; existing
process variables take precedence. No sensitive file is rewritten.
Archive passwords use --passphrase-file FILE, '-' for stdin, or a confirmed hidden
prompt. Catalog secrets use GLESHA_CATALOG_PASSPHRASE or --catalog-passphrase-file.
Snapshots with different passwords accept --archive-passphrase-file ID=FILE overrides.
.SH FILES
registry.db stores set and remote identities. Each set UUID has a catalog.db.
Catalogs contain metadata and hashes, never file content. Local browsing makes no
cloud requests. Remote catalogs are encrypted by default and use S3 Standard.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
glesha create docs ~/Documents \-\-to b2

glesha run docs \-\-env /secure/cloud.env

glesha run docs \-\-incremental \-\-env /secure/cloud.env

glesha browse docs \-\-path Documents \-\-recursive

glesha restore docs \-\-into ~/recovered

glesha archive ~/Documents \-\-compression gzip

glesha import old\-docs \-\-from b2 \-\-key backups/encb\-old.tar.gz.gpg

glesha help run
.fi
.SH OUTPUT
Colors default to auto: enabled only on a terminal. Use --color=yes to force colors,
or --color=no to disable them. The earlier always and never names remain aliases.
Interactive stderr displays a progress bar, readable byte counts and average speed.
Progress is disabled for redirected stderr, JSON output and warn/error/silent logging.
Scanning uses an ASCII spinner (minus, backslash, vertical bar, slash).
Archiving shows processed/total uncompressed file bytes and a percentage.
Finalization and metadata publication show status without an indefinite bar. Bars refresh every 100 ms, use # for filled
progress and adapt to about 80 percent of terminal width. Upload progress includes
in-flight payload bytes; checksum reads and retries do not count bytes twice. Bytes reaching 100 percent do not imply
that final validation or catalog publication has finished.
Use --log-level=debug for operation and worker-budget diagnostics.
Progress counters use compact binary units: [63.35%] [1.27G/2.34G] [1.22M/s].
Explicit worker flags show the effective worker count; archiving and individual
downloads use one worker. Other human sizes use KiB, MiB and GiB. JSON retains integer byte counts.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Existing encb gzip/OpenPGP archives are read without recompression or replacement.
Compression is detected from the decrypted stream, independent of filenames.
Passwords are never stored. Archive passwords and the catalog secret are separate.
Database and JSON formats are versioned. Unsupported catalog schemas are rejected
without modifying the file; old catalogs are neither migrated nor deleted implicitly.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0: success. 1: operation failure. 2: invalid usage or missing selection.
3: pending work or cold retrieval. 4: conflicting remote history.
